cartridge "GIVE 'N' TAKE/COMPUTE'S GAZETTE/1987"

' GIVE 'N' TAKE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Give 'N' Take", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk,
' August 1987 (Commodore 64, BASIC with a small machine-language character
' copier; no author named: the listing carries only the COMPUTE!
' Publications copyright). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a jigsaw board of 70 regions in 18 shapes. The 70 pieces (one
' per region, the same shapes) are dealt at random, 35 to each player.
' Players take turns; a turn places one of your pieces on a free region of
' the same shape. Every neighbouring region the opponent holds turns to
' your colour. After 70 turns, TAKE: the most regions wins; GIVE: the
' fewest regions wins. Equal counts tie.
'
' From the original (orig/give _n_ take.bas): the board, its 70 regions,
' their shapes and neighbour lists (DATA 180-370, 560-700, A$(1)-A$(18);
' board.py rebuilds the board from them and checks the neighbour table
' against the cells: no mismatch), the region outlines drawn with the
' listing's own edge characters (DATA 440-490), the random 35/35 deal
' (lines 720-740), the move rule and the flip of the opponent's neighbours
' (lines 900-960), a refused move buzzes (line 1670), both hands shown
' beside the board with a count per shape (lines 1070-1180), the score as
' regions held, GIVE or TAKE chosen first (lines 1260-1270, the winner
' test inverted for GIVE at line 990), 1 or 2 players, the computer moving
' first or second (line 90), player 1 opens, 70 turns, the tie. The
' computer is the listing's (lines 1590-1660): it lists every legal region
' in board order and picks one at random.
'
' Liberties: the cursor moves over the board with the stick (to the next
' region in that direction) instead of stepping through the regions in
' number order with left/right; the region under the cursor is lit and a
' line under the board names its shape, its owner and how many of that
' shape the side to move holds; free regions that the side to move can
' play are drawn white, the others grey. A second computer level, GREEDY,
' takes the move that turns the most regions (TAKE) or the fewest (GIVE),
' ties at random; RANDOM is the listing's. The computer walks the cursor
' to its region at a human pace. Captured regions turn one by one with a
' sound. Players and options are chosen with the stick, not the keys G/T/1/2;
' either controller moves for the side to move (the listing's one-joystick
' option). Game end: the message, then a press (or 30 seconds) goes back to
' the title, instead of the listing's RUN on the button.
'
' HOW TO PLAY (title menu): two pages of plain rules.
'
' Controls: the stick moves the cursor, a button places a piece. On the
' title, up and down choose, left/right or a button change an option, a
' button on START GAME starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' screens drawn with the NMI off after a wait, display off; music first in
' the sound table; the title tune is played once; `set sprite count` is
' always given. Nothing flashes.

